> The \emph{text reader} does no arithmetic: it splits the trace at each option's mention and takes the last result number written in that option's segment, skipping the inputs. On 150 traces annotated by two LLM annotators who did not see our outputs (a third relabeled 60 and agreed on every option; LLM labels are a check, not ground truth), it finds 96\% of the stated values and reads 95\% correctly; the second parser below overrides its misreads. A second parser marks an option \emph{evaluated} when the trace states its true expected profit within 2\% (92\% precision, 97\% recall per option against the annotators). An option's written value is its true value when the parser finds it and the reader's number otherwise, so a value counts as miscomputed only if the true one appears nowhere. The split in Table~\ref{tab:traces} therefore rests mostly on exact numeric matches to computed expected profits (97--100\% of written values for five models, 86\% for Granite-8B); the LLM annotations only estimate how often the matcher misses a value written in another form. Miscomputed values are 14\% of Granite-8B's written values and at most 3\% for the others, against the annotators' 16\% and $\le$1\%.
